    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d624">

                <pb facs="0798" n="704" sameAs="frus:frus1896/pg704"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Olney to Mr. Taylor.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1896-12-15"><hi rendition="#italic">December 15,
                        1896</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 625.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I inclose copies of two letters<anchor
                        n="1" xml:id="d624-f1"/> from the chairman of the Juragua Iron Company,
                    Limited, stating that that company has been threatened by the Cuban insurgents
                    with destruction of the works unless they should have paid by the 30th ultimo an
                    indemnity fund of $1,000,000 and export dues amounting to $3,000 per month on
                    ore shipped.</p>
                <p>You will bring this matter to the attention of the Spanish Government, and
                    request that it may give immediate and complete protection to the company’s
                    property against any attempt on the part of the insurgents to carry out the
                    above-mentioned threat.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Richard Olney.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
